%S A398564 1,2,2,3,6,3,4,4,12,8,5,10,15,20,5,6,6,6,12,30,6,7,14,21,28,35,42,7,8,
%T A398564 8,24,16,40,24,56,32,9,18,9,36,45,18,63,72,27,10,10,30,20,10,30,70,40,
%U A398564 90,10,11,22,33,44,55,66,77,88,99,110,11,12,12,12,24,60,12,84,48,36,60,132,24
%N A398564 Triangle read by rows: T(n,k) is the greatest common divisor of the numbers of ways to form j simultaneous cross-team handshakes between teams of n and k distinct people, with each person participating in at most one handshake, for 1 <= j <= k <= n.
%C A398564 The handshakes are matchings in the complete bipartite graph K_{n,k}, whose two parts represent teams of n and k distinct people. Each matching consists of disjoint pairs, with one person from each team. There are j!*binomial(n,j)*binomial(k,j) matchings of size j.
%C A398564 The gcd of these matching counts for j = 1..k is T(n,k) = n*k/rad(gcd(n,k)), where rad(m) is the product of the distinct prime factors of m. Thus T(n,k) = n*k when gcd(n,k) = 1, and T(n,k) = lcm(n,k) when gcd(n,k) is squarefree.
%C A398564 This triangle is also connected to the weighted permutation polynomials C_m(X) studied in Kallat (2026). Here C_m(X) is the sum over all permutations of m elements, with each permutation weighted by the product of its cycle weights. A cycle of length r contributes a factor X_r. The gcd of the integer coefficients of C_{n+k}(X) - C_n(X)*C_k(X) is exactly T(n,k). The coefficient-content identity underlies universal shift congruences for a family of weighted-permutation sequences. The associated framework yields applications to 101 existing OEIS sequences (Kallat, 2026).
%C A398564 The gcd of these coefficients need not equal the gcd of all values obtained by assigning integer values to the cycle weights.
%C A398564 Let M(n,k) be the gcd of all integer values of C_{n+k}(X) - C_n(X)*C_k(X) obtained by assigning integers to the cycle weights.
%C A398564 Open question: Can M(n,k) be determined explicitly, and for which pairs (n,k) does M(n,k) > T(n,k)?
%H A398564 Ahaan Kallat, <a href="/A398564/b398564.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1225</a>
%H A398564 Ahaan Kallat, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15849">Universal Shift Congruences for Weighted Permutation Sequences</a>, arXiv:2610.15849 [math.CO], 2026.
%F A398564 T(n,k) = n*k/A007947(gcd(n,k)).
%F A398564 T(n,k) = gcd_{j=1..k} (j!*binomial(n,j)*binomial(k,j)).
%F A398564 T(n,k) = content(C_{n+k}(X) - C_n(X)*C_k(X)), where content is the gcd of the integer coefficients and C_m(X) is defined in Comments.
%F A398564 T(n,1) = n.
%F A398564 T(n,n) = n^2/rad(n).
%F A398564 T(n,k)/lcm(n,k) = gcd(n,k)/rad(gcd(n,k)).
%F A398564 gcd_{k=1..n} T(n,k) = n.
%F A398564 T(n,k) = gcd(n*k, {p!*binomial(n,p)*binomial(k,p) : p prime, p | gcd(n,k)}).
%e A398564 Rows n = 1..6 are
%e A398564   1;
%e A398564   2, 2;
%e A398564   3, 6, 3;
%e A398564   4, 4, 12, 8;
%e A398564   5, 10, 15, 20, 5;
%e A398564   6, 6, 6, 12, 30, 6.
%e A398564 For two teams of four distinct people each, there are 16 ways to form exactly one handshake, 72 ways to form exactly two, 96 ways to form exactly three, and 24 ways to form exactly four. The greatest common divisor of 16, 72, 96, and 24 is 8, so T(4,4) = 8.
%o A398564 (Python)
%o A398564 from math import gcd, prod
%o A398564 from sympy import factorint
%o A398564 def T(n, k):
%o A398564     return n*k // prod(factorint(gcd(n, k)))
%o A398564 print([T(n, k) for n in range(1, 13) for k in range(1, n+1)])
%Y A398564 Cf. A007947 (radical of an integer), A374443 and A374433 (related radical-of-gcd arrays), and A086885 (bipartite matchings).
%Y A398564 Cf. A000085 (involutions) and A000166 (derangements) for weighted-permutation specializations of the associated polynomial theorem.
%K A398564 nonn,tabl,new
%O A398564 1,2
